      {
         "id": "SECTION_carry",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Remembering the Carry and the Complement of 10\n\nBefore using the tools, let us remember the two ideas that make addition work.\n\nThe **complement to 10** of a digit is how much is still missing to reach ten. The complement of 9 is 1, the complement of 6 is 4, and the complement of 5 is 5.\n\nWhen the sum reaches ten, we group those ten units into one unit of the next order. That group is the **carry**, also called *advancing an order*. The digit that stays in the column is what is left outside the group of ten.\n\n**Example: 9 + 3.** The complement of 9 is 1. Because 3 is greater than or equal to 1, the ten can be completed: we take 1 from the 3, carry 1 to the next order, and 2 remain (3 - 1 = 2). Therefore, 9 + 3 = 12.\n\n<div class=\"htAddSquares\"><span class=\"htAddTenGroup\"><span class=\"htAddSquare htAddSquareBase\"></span><span class=\"htAddSquare htAddSquareBase\"></span><span class=\"htAddSquare htAddSquareBase\"></span><span class=\"htAddSquare htAddSquareBase\"></span><span class=\"htAddSquare htAddSquareBase\"></span><span class=\"htAddSquare htAddSquareBase\"></span><span class=\"htAddSquare htAddSquareBase\"></span><span class=\"htAddSquare htAddSquareBase\"></span><span class=\"htAddSquare htAddSquareBase\"></span><span class=\"htAddSquare htAddSquareAdded\"></span></span><span class=\"htAddSquare htAddSquareAdded\"></span><span class=\"htAddSquare htAddSquareAdded\"></span></div>\n<span class=\"htAddSquaresCaption\">9 + 3 = 12</span>\n<span class=\"htAddSquaresNote\">The dashed group is the ten: it becomes the 1 carried to the next order. The 2 squares outside the group stay in the units column.</span>\n\nEach tool represents the group of ten in its own way: a new knot in the next order, a horizontal bar, a bead in the next column, or a marker in the next row. This way, every round practices the carry and the complement of 10 (<htcite0>; <htcite1>; <htcite2>).",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "cabc9843-35ef-4e4f-92fd-17eda864b55e",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Advancing an Order and the Complement",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2026",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "687a38b5-de0e-4d16-a4d0-8ec87a545ccb",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Running Among the Numbers",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2026",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "5539096c-dd7d-4f9c-a137-f0465725b9f4",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Carry or Not Carry? (Game)",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2026",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 1: Why does the carry appear?</b><br /><br />When the sum reaches ten, why do we group ten units into one unit of the next order?<br /><br />What would happen if we never made that group?</div></div></center></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
